---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const hero = '/media-studio/studio-library-20261114.jpg';
const init = '/media-studio/init-media-20261114.jpg';
const sound = '/media-studio/studio-sound-20261114.jpg';
const storyboard = '/media-studio/studio-storyboard-20261114.jpg';
const audioAsset = '/media-studio/studio-audio-20261114.jpg';
const publicPage = '/media-studio/studio-public-20261114.jpg';

const chapters = [
  ['target', 'Understand the target app'],
  ['accounts', 'Accounts and the supported agent'],
  ['ready', 'Get Ready'],
  ['init', 'Choose Media Studio in Init'],
  ['handoff', 'Save and hand off the brief'],
  ['build', 'The agent builds and deploys'],
  ['enter', 'Enter through Composer'],
  ['make', 'Create and edit video and sound'],
  ['publish', 'Assemble, export and publish'],
  ['return', 'Return and change it'],
  ['technical', 'Technical reference'],
];

const promptBrief = 'Build me a media studio for images, video and sound using my providers, with projects I can save and return to. Give it its own Cloudflare account resources, its own storage and its own release, and open it from my App Composer menu.';

const promptSound = 'A slow instrumental bed for a short studio film: warm analog pad, tape hiss, a patient four-note motif, brushed low percussion, no vocals, clean ending.';

const promptAgent = 'Use the saved Media Studio specification and the pinned source in this handoff. Create my own copy, apply every migration in order, set the secrets yourself without printing them, deploy, and read back the live revision. Then walk me through the one account step only I can do.';
---
<Base
  title="Build and run your own media studio with App Composer"
  description="A repeatable, illustrated tutorial: brief, Get Ready, Init, agent handoff, deployment, native access, real media work, assembly, publication and changing the same studio afterwards."
  shareImage="https://composer.loftwah.com/media-studio/media-studio-og-20261114.jpg"
  shareAlt="The Media Studio library with an assembled cut and generated sound."
  ogType="article"
  canonical="https://composer.loftwah.com/media-studio/tutorial"
>
<article class="tut">
  <header class="tut-head">
    <p class="kicker">Media Studio · tutorial</p>
    <h1>Build and run your own media studio with App Composer</h1>
    <p class="deck">The same recipe as the <a href="/media-studio">build article</a>, chapter by chapter. Each chapter says what you do, what your agent or the product does, what you should see, and how to recover if it goes wrong.</p>
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>You need</dt><dd>A Cloudflare account on Workers Paid, a supported agent, and a funded fal.ai key for video, sound and rendering.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>You do not need</dt><dd>Docker, Wrangler, manual migrations or a database seed. The agent performs the technical work.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Cost</dt><dd>Hosting plus per-request inference. This run used US$0.2035 of inference for sound, two assemblies and a portrait cut.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Time</dt><dd>An evening for a first build including account setup and the first render; minutes for later changes.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <nav class="jump" aria-label="Chapters">
      {chapters.map(([id, label], index) => (
        <a href={`#${id}`}>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')} {label}</a>
      ))}
    </nav>
    <p class="small">The walkthrough film follows these chapters. Timestamps below refer to <a href="#film">the recording</a>.</p>
  </header>

  <section id="target" class="step">
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>What you do</dt><dd>Decide what the studio is for and where its data will live.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What the agent or product does</dt><dd>Composer holds the brief; the studio app owns the projects and media.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What you should see</dt><dd>A clear answer about ownership before any account work.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="kicker">01 · Understand the target app</p>
    <h2>What you are actually building</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>A media studio that belongs to you: an MIT-licensed Cloudflare Worker with its own D1 database for projects, assets, jobs, budgets and compositions, its own R2 bucket for originals and assembled exports, and its own release. It is not a Composer feature inside Composer's database.</p>
        <p>Composer is the front door and the brief. The studio is the building. Models are hired services: Cloudflare Workers AI for stills, fal.ai for video, sound and bounded renders.</p>
        <p>What you own at the end: the source, the data, the media, the publication pages and the ability to change any of it.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>Checkpoint</h3>
        <p>You can say where your projects will live (your Cloudflare account), where the media will live (your R2 bucket) and which vendor is paid for each kind of request. If any answer is "Composer", stop and read the specification again.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="accounts" class="step">
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>What you do</dt><dd>Create or sign in to Cloudflare, fund fal.ai, start your agent.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What the agent or product does</dt><dd>The agent prepares the technical environment; it never creates accounts or spends for you.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What you should see</dt><dd>A funded provider balance and an agent that can run commands.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="kicker">02 · Accounts and the supported agent</p>
    <h2>The parts only you can do</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>Agents should not create accounts, accept terms or spend money on your behalf. You do these once:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Create or sign in to your Cloudflare account and confirm Workers Paid with Containers if you intend to use the Oyster route. The independent studio itself needs Workers Paid and Workers AI.</li>
          <li>Create a fal.ai account and add credit. Video, sound and assembly all bill there.</li>
          <li>Start a supported agent (for example a coding agent with terminal access) in an empty project directory and connect it to Composer's Get Ready.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Then say this to your agent:</p>
        <pre><code>{promptAgent}</code></pre>
        <p>The consent boundary is simple: you approve account creation, sign-in and spend. The agent handles repositories, configuration, migrations, tests and deployment. It must never print a secret; it writes secrets directly to the provider.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>Checkpoint</h3>
        <p>You can see a funded fal.ai balance and your Cloudflare account has Workers AI available. No key has been pasted into chat, a prompt, a screenshot or a repository.</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p><strong>No fal key yet?</strong> Everything except video, sound and rendering still works: uploads, the library, stills on Workers AI, export and publication. Add the key when you are ready.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="ready" class="step">
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>What you do</dt><dd>Run Get Ready and read the report.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What the agent or product does</dt><dd>Checks the runtime, package manager, repository tools and accounts.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What you should see</dt><dd>Every check named, with a real pass or fail.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="kicker">03 · Get Ready</p>
    <h2>Check the machine before building</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p><a href="/init">Init</a> includes Get Ready. It checks the local runtime, the package manager, the repository tools and the accounts your agent will use. It reports what is missing rather than pretending a tool exists.</p>
        <p>Read the result. If it says a runtime is unsupported, fix that first: a release gate that runs on the wrong Node line fails in confusing ways later.</p>
        <p class="small">Get Ready is not a subscription and it provisions nothing in your cloud account.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>What you should see</h3>
        <p>A readiness report with each check named and a clear pass or fail. No green tick means no green tick.</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p>A failing local check is a local fix, not a product bug: install the supported runtime line, re-run Get Ready, and only then continue.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="init" class="step">
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>What you do</dt><dd>Choose Media Studio, set the defaults, write the brief.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What the agent or product does</dt><dd>Validates the draft and builds a portable specification.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What you should see</dt><dd>A live preview and a spec panel that updates with your choices.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="kicker">04 · Choose Media Studio in Init</p>
    <h2>The brief</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>Open <a href="/init?start=media-studio">Init → Media Studio</a>, then choose what the studio should make, the still and video models, the default frame and clip length, and the sound model and bed length. Name the project. None of this buys anything.</p>
        <p>Write the intent in your own words. The sentence used for this build:</p>
        <pre><code>{promptBrief}</code></pre>
        <p>The saved specification is portable and validated. Revision 2 carries the sound defaults; revision 1 drafts still load.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>What you should see</h3>
        <p>The Media Studio fieldset with default values, a live preview, and a "Portable specification" panel that updates as you change your choices.</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p>If a value is rejected, the form tells you which field and why. Fix it there: the specification fails closed rather than shipping an unsupported model id.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={init} width="1600" height="1000" alt="The Media Studio section of the Init form with name, focus, still and video models, default frame, clip length, sound model and sound length." loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Init's Media Studio section on the live page. The sound fields are part of revision 2 of the specification; revision 1 drafts still load.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="handoff" class="step">
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>What you do</dt><dd>Save the draft, export the spec, copy the agent handoff.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What the agent or product does</dt><dd>Pins the source commit and lists the bindings, build plan and human-only actions.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What you should see</dt><dd>A JSON file that round-trips, and a handoff naming an exact commit.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="kicker">05 · Save and hand off the brief</p>
    <h2>Make the contract portable</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>Save the draft, download the specification, and copy the agent handoff. The handoff pins the studio repository and commit, names the storage and secret bindings, states the build plan and separates the human-only actions from the technical ones.</p>
        <p>Keep the draft. Exporting does not publish anything, and importing a different specification later does not overwrite your saved draft until you choose to save it.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>What you should see</h3>
        <p>A JSON file you can re-import with identical values, and a handoff that names an exact forty-character commit — not "latest".</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p>If the handoff shows a placeholder commit, the source pin is wrong. Stop; a moving target cannot be reproduced.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <p class="small">The <a href="/media-studio/media-studio-brief-20261114.json" download>example brief</a> from this run is available as a starting point.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="build" class="step">
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>What you do</dt><dd>Approve the one account step only you can do.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What the agent or product does</dt><dd>Creates your copy, applies migrations in order, sets secrets, deploys and reads back the revision.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What you should see</dt><dd>A live /healthz that returns the commit your agent shipped.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="kicker">06 · The agent builds and deploys</p>
    <h2>Owner-controlled work</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>Your agent creates your copy of the studio, applies every migration in order, sets the secrets, deploys and reads back the live revision. On this run the release script refused a dirty tree, checked the pinned source, ran the focused tests, deployed, then compared <code>/healthz</code> to the commit it had shipped.</p>
        <p>Two things to watch, because they are where real builds fail:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Migrations are ordered and data-preserving.</strong> The audio release rebuilds two tables to widen a constraint; it copies every row and restores parent links in a second pass, because the production database enforces foreign keys.</li>
          <li><strong>The handoff expiry must match units.</strong> Composer mints a short-lived token; the studio compares milliseconds. A mismatch rejects a correct handoff as expired. Both sides now normalise.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>What you should see</h3>
        <p><code>/healthz</code> returning your exact commit, and the studio's login page offering the Composer route rather than only a passphrase.</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p><strong>Deploy refused:</strong> read the refusal — dirty tree, wrong branch, unpushed commit, or a manifest that does not match the repository you are deploying from. Fix that, not the checks.</p>
        <p><strong>Foreign key error in a migration:</strong> do not delete data to get past it. Rebuild the parent first, then the children, then drop the old parent.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="enter" class="step">
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>What you do</dt><dd>Open the studio from the Composer menu.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What the agent or product does</dt><dd>Exchanges the EmDash session for a short-lived studio handoff.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What you should see</dt><dd>Your projects, and private routes that return 401 signed out.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="kicker">07 · Enter through Composer</p>
    <h2>No second password</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>Open the studio from the Composer menu. Composer's existing EmDash session mints a short-lived handoff and the studio exchanges it for its own owner session. The browser holds neither the studio passphrase nor any provider key.</p>
        <p>Signing in is not the same as being granted a resource: the studio's private APIs check the owner session on the server. A signed-out request to a private route returns 401, not a hidden menu item.</p>
        <p>Standalone use still exists behind the owner passphrase, because the studio is an ordinary independent app. It is simply not the way you arrive from Composer.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>What you should see</h3>
        <p>The studio's own header with the App Composer attribution, your projects, and a monthly allowance strip in the studio's currency, not Composer's.</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p><strong>"This Composer handoff is not valid or has expired":</strong> reopen it from the menu rather than reusing an old URL; handoffs are deliberately short-lived.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={hero} width="1600" height="1000" alt="The studio library with an assembled cut, two waveform sound cards and three takes." loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The library, opened from Composer: one assembled cut, two generated sound assets and the takes they came from.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="make" class="step">
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>What you do</dt><dd>Pick a model, write the direction, confirm the estimate.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What the agent or product does</dt><dd>Sends one durable request, then saves the owned result with provenance and cost.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What you should see</dt><dd>A saved still, clip or audio asset — sound with a real waveform and duration.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="kicker">08 · Create and edit video and sound</p>
    <h2>Make one real thing at a time</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>Upload something first. An image, a clip and a piece of sound all land in the same project with real dimensions, duration and hashes. Then, in <strong>Create</strong>:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Pick a still model and a frame. Read the estimate before you confirm.</li>
          <li>To animate, choose a saved still and one motion instruction. Video is asynchronous: you can close the browser.</li>
          <li>For sound, choose <strong>Make sound</strong>, pick Studio Score or Studio Voice, write the direction and choose a length.</li>
        </ol>
        <pre><code>{promptSound}</code></pre>
        <p>The finished sound is saved as audio, not attached to a video: duration, sample rate, channels and provenance are recorded, and the waveform is drawn from the decoded file.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>What you should see</h3>
        <p>A job in History, then a saved asset. For sound, a playable card with a waveform and its real duration.</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p><strong>"This route needs a fal.ai key":</strong> the key is missing on the studio, not in your shell. Add it to the deployment's secrets.</p>
        <p><strong>Provider refuses for funding:</strong> nothing was charged. Add credit on the studio's own provider account and retry the same request.</p>
        <p><strong>Save failed after the provider finished:</strong> use "Retry saving". It writes the retained bytes and does not generate again. Never mint a new request id to fix a save.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={sound} width="1440" height="900" alt="Create tab with Make sound selected, Studio Score, a written direction, a 17-second length and an estimate." loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>One estimated request, with the length chosen to fit the cut.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={audioAsset} width="1440" height="900" alt="A saved narration asset with waveform, player, duration, sample rate, channels and hash." loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Saved narration: real waveform, real metadata, publishable on its own page.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="publish" class="step">
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>What you do</dt><dd>Order the takes, add the bed, render, then publish deliberately.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What the agent or product does</dt><dd>Signs short-lived read links, composes one bounded export, copies it into your bucket.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What you should see</dt><dd>An assembled MP4 with real duration and take count; a public page that loads signed out.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="kicker">09 · Assemble, export and publish</p>
    <h2>The cut is the product</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>Open <strong>Assemble</strong>. Add two to six saved takes in order, choose one bed, and save the storyboard. Rendering is one bounded provider composition; the result is copied into your own storage. A 9:16 cut composes the same takes portrait-first.</p>
        <p>Publication is a separate, deliberate act. Four controls do four different things:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Publish / unpublish</strong> — a public page exists or is withdrawn. Withdrawal stops new visits; it cannot recall a download already taken.</li>
          <li><strong>Show / hide</strong> — a card placement. Hiding removes discovery without making the direct link private.</li>
          <li><strong>Make private</strong> — the media stops being available on public pages; the originals stay in your library.</li>
          <li><strong>Delete</strong> — destructive and separate. It removes the bytes and tombstones the publications that used them.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>What you should see</h3>
        <p>An assembled MP4 with its dimensions, real duration and take count; a public page that loads signed out; private routes that still return 401.</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p><strong>A render that ends in "result unavailable":</strong> reconcile the job. It re-reads the same provider result; it does not dispatch a new one.</p>
        <p><strong>A 9:16 cut refused:</strong> the storyboard has no portrait take. Add one, or accept the wide cut.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={storyboard} width="1440" height="900" alt="The Assemble tab with a saved storyboard, render controls and the assembled result." loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The storyboard and its render, in the same project that holds the takes.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={publicPage} width="1440" height="900" alt="A public Media Studio page with the assembled cut, description, player and download." loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>A published page, signed out. The example is live at <a href="https://media.loftwah.com/p/Na9FNUB_VhzZ7zA9">media.loftwah.com</a>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="return" class="step">
    <dl class="roles">
      <div><dt>What you do</dt><dd>Ask for a change you actually want.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What the agent or product does</dt><dd>The same agent changes the same application and releases it.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>What you should see</dt><dd>The new behaviour, with earlier projects, cuts and renders still there.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p class="kicker">10 · Return and change it</p>
    <h2>The part that makes it yours</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <p>Come back with a real annoyance, not a rename. In this run it was two: the Assemble tab could not see saved storyboards, and the audio bed was always thirty seconds. The agent changed the same application, the release shipped, and the projects stayed put.</p>
        <p>After that release: choose a bed length of 10 to 30 seconds, edit a storyboard in place, and see when a cut has changed since its last render. Earlier renders stay attached.</p>
        <p>Then verify the claim rather than trusting it: reopen the project, play the earlier cut, play the new one, and compare durations. Reopening reads stored bytes; it does not generate again.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="check">
        <h3>What you should see</h3>
        <p>Two renders in one composition, 30.00 s and 17.02 s, both downloadable, with the takes unchanged.</p>
        <h3>Recover</h3>
        <p><strong>The change did not appear:</strong> check the live revision against the commit. A green build is not a deployment.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

